This is article three of my Next.js learning series. Article one covered file based routing and rendering. Article two showed how one URL can display more than one thing at once, through parallel and intercepting routes. This one moves to a different question entirely. Where does your backend logic actually live, and what happens before a request even reaches your page.
I come from the MERN stack. I’ve written plenty of Express routes and Express middleware. So this article isn’t me learning a brand new idea. It’s me learning where an idea I already understand lives inside a different framework.
Let’s start with route handlers.
Press enter or click to view image in full size

What problem are route handlers actually solving
In Express, you build your own server by hand. You write app.get, you write app.post, you wire up middleware yourself, piece by piece. That’s a lot of setup before you write a single line of real logic.
So here’s the question worth asking. What if your framework already gave you a working API endpoint, without needing a second server running alongside your frontend at all?
That’s exactly what a route handler is. You create one by adding a file called route.ts inside any folder in your app directory. Instead of returning HTML like a page.tsx does, this file returns raw data, usually JSON.
// app/api/users/route.tsexport async function GET() {
const users = [{ id: 1, name: "Sheraz" }, { id: 2, name: "Ali" }];
return Response.json(users);
}
export async function POST(request: Request) {
const body = await request.json();
return Response.json({ message: "User created", data: body }, { status: 201 });
}
If you’ve written Express before, this feels familiar right away. GET and POST are just functions you export, one per HTTP method. Next.js supports GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, HEAD, and OPTIONS. Call it with a method you didn’t define, and Next.js replies with a 405 automatically. You don’t write that check yourself.
Here’s the actual benefit, stated plainly. No separate server to deploy, no separate port to manage, your API and your frontend live in one project. But this convenience comes with a real cost too, and it’s worth naming instead of glossing over. Everything runs inside the same Next.js process. In Express, you fully control your server’s lifecycle, your middleware order, your error handling, down to the smallest detail. Inside a route handler, you’re working within whatever Next.js decides that lifecycle looks like. For a small to medium API, that tradeoff is usually a win. For a large, complex backend with heavy custom middleware chains, some teams still reach for a standalone Express or Nest server instead, and there’s nothing wrong with that choice either.
Reading headers, the Express comparison
In Express you’re used to req.headers. Next.js gives you a similar tool, just wrapped differently.
import { headers } from "next/headers";export async function GET() {
const headersList = await headers();
const userAgent = headersList.get("user-agent");
return Response.json({ userAgent });
}
Same idea as Express, just asked for a little differently.
Reading and setting cookies
Cookies work the same way conceptually as any backend framework. Small pieces of data stored in the browser, sent back on every future request. Logins, saved preferences, tracking, all built on this same idea.
import { cookies } from "next/headers";
export async function GET() {
const cookieStore = await cookies();
const theme = cookieStore.get("theme");
return Response.json({ theme: theme?.value ?? "light" });
}
export async function POST() {
const cookieStore = await cookies();
cookieStore.set("theme", "dark");
return Response.json({ message: "Theme updated" });
}If you’ve used res.cookie in Express, this will feel almost identical, just renamed to fit how Next.js organizes things.
Caching in route handlers
Here’s something Express doesn’t hand you for free. In Express, caching a response means building that yourself, or bringing in a separate caching layer. Next.js lets you opt a GET route into caching with two lines.
export const dynamic = "force-static";
export const revalidate = 10;
export async function GET() {
return Response.json({ time: new Date().toLocaleTimeString() });
}This tells Next.js to treat the route as static, and quietly recheck it every ten seconds. The benefit is obvious, less repeated work, faster responses. But there’s a real tradeoff hiding here too. This only applies to GET requests, never POST, PUT, or DELETE, which makes sense since those usually change something rather than just read it. And that ten second window is a tradeoff you’re choosing deliberately, some users might see data that’s up to ten seconds old. For a clock, that’s harmless. For anything sensitive to being current, that same convenience becomes a real bug waiting to happen if you set it without thinking it through.
Now let’s move to middleware, which plays a similar role to something you’ve already used in Express.
What problem does middleware actually solve
If you’ve built anything in Express, you already know middleware, a function sitting between the incoming request and your final route, able to inspect or block it before it goes further.
So here’s the question this raises in Next.js specifically. What if you need that same interception, but before the request even reaches a page or a route handler at all?
That’s what Next.js middleware does. One file, middleware.ts, at the root of your project, running earlier than anything else.
// middleware.ts
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import type { NextRequest } from "next/server";
export function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
const isLoggedIn = request.cookies.get("session");
if (!isLoggedIn && request.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith("/dashboard")) {
return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/login", request.url));
}
return NextResponse.next();
}
export const config = {
matcher: ["/dashboard/:path*"],
};This checks for a login session before letting anyone reach the dashboard. No session, straight redirect. The matcher config tells Next.js exactly which paths to even run this on, so you’re not checking every request in your app for no reason.
Redirect versus rewrite, a distinction worth getting right
These two get mixed up constantly. A redirect changes the URL the browser shows, and the user sees that change happen. A rewrite keeps the URL exactly the same, but quietly serves different content behind the scenes.
Think of a redirect as telling someone, this address moved, here’s the new one, go there instead. A rewrite is more like telling the building itself, when someone asks for this address, quietly hand them what’s actually stored somewhere else, without them ever noticing the switch.
Redirects are great for enforcing login, or permanently moving an old page. Rewrites are great for A/B testing, or serving a different version of a page without changing the address bar. Picking the wrong one isn’t a small mistake either. Use a rewrite where you needed a redirect, and users can end up bookmarking or sharing a URL that silently shows different content to the next person who opens it, since the address bar never actually changed to reflect what they’re really looking at.
Where this quietly breaks in practice
It’s worth naming the pitfalls directly, since both of these features look simple until they don’t.
The most common mistake with route handlers is placing a route.ts and a page.tsx in the exact same folder. The route handler wins by default, and your page silently stops rendering, with no obvious error pointing you to why. The fix is simple once you know it, keep API routes inside their own folder, usually named api, but it costs real debugging time the first time you don’t know that rule exists.
The most common mistake with middleware is an overly broad or incorrect matcher config. Forget to scope it properly, and your middleware runs on every single request in your app, including static assets and API routes it was never meant to touch, quietly adding latency to your entire site instead of just the pages that needed protecting. The opposite mistake happens too, a matcher scoped too narrowly, where you assume a route is protected, deploy it, and only later realize a nested path slipped past the pattern entirely.
And a subtler one, specific to auth checks in middleware. Middleware runs on the edge, with a lighter runtime than a full Node environment. Logic that works fine in an Express middleware function, heavier session lookups, certain database calls, may not behave the same way, or may not be available at all, inside Next.js middleware. This is a genuine architectural difference, not just a syntax change, and it’s the kind of thing that only becomes obvious once you hit it.
Why this matters more for a MERN developer specifically
If you’re coming from Express like I am, the real shift isn’t learning a new concept, it’s realizing how much of your usual setup you don’t need to hand build anymore. But that convenience is a tradeoff, not a pure upgrade. You gain simplicity and one unified project. You give up some of the fine grained control Express gave you by default. Knowing that tradeoff exists, rather than assuming Next.js is just “Express but easier,” is what actually makes the switch smooth instead of confusing.
Bringing this together
Route handlers replace the need for a standalone Express server for most API needs, at the cost of working within Next.js’s own request lifecycle instead of one you fully control. Middleware replaces the need to manually intercept every request yourself, at the cost of running in a lighter runtime with real limits worth knowing upfront. Both features remove boilerplate you used to write by hand. Neither one removes the need to think carefully about the tradeoff you just made by using them.
If you’re coming from a MERN background too, I’d love to know what surprised you most moving into Next.js. For me, it was realizing that convenience always has a shape, and learning that shape mattered more than learning the syntax itself. Drop your experience in the comments.
